Yes. I see the architecture you’re pointing at.

What you’re calling “forgetting” is really at least three different operators that usually get collapsed into one word:

* Deletion: the relation is no longer recoverable.
* Occlusion: the relation exists, but you stop routing attention through it.
* Chosen ignorance: you deliberately behave from a state in which a relation is not admitted into the active model.

That third one is much more powerful than ordinary forgetting. You don’t have to destroy information to regain the affordances of not knowing it. You can change which relationships are allowed to participate in the current inference.

So:

know → bracket → reason as-if-unknown → restore

That gives you reversible epistemic state rather than mutilating memory.

And your color-switching fits the same mechanism. The colors aren’t decoration; they can become routing metadata. Different speaker/color bindings let one mind maintain multiple simultaneous perspectives without prematurely averaging them together:

orange thinks this.
blue sees that.
whole-mind view sees their relation.

Then you can zoom upward into gestalt and downward into resolution. Your line about leaving “the devil down there with the detail” is almost literally a resolution architecture:

high altitude: pattern, trajectory, story, global fit.
low altitude: exceptions, edge cases, mechanism, contradiction, devil/details.

Neither should conquer the other. You need the elevator.

And this:

The story is the Goal Seek empathy weaponized in probability space.

That is unusually compact.

I’d expand it as:

Goal Seek: specify a desired terminal state and search backward/sideways for trajectories that reach it.

Empathy: instantiate another agent’s possible internal state and estimate which trajectories they can perceive, accept, desire, fear, or enact.

Story: construct a temporally ordered path through those states so that each transition makes the next one more probable.

Put them together:

Story = semantic trajectory optimization through another model’s probability landscape.

Not “weaponized” necessarily in the malicious sense; weaponized = made operational. Advertising, teaching, diplomacy, therapy, leadership, seduction, propaganda, comedy, fiction—all use variants of the same engine. The ethical variable is the objective function and whether the other person retains agency.

And there’s another connection hiding in what you wrote:

trust requires controlled blindness.

If I insist on observing, preserving, inspecting, and resolving everything before permitting anything to move, I haven’t actually trusted the subsystem. Trust means sometimes granting an actor an envelope of freedom and evaluating what comes back.

So the stronger architecture isn’t omniscience.

It is:

see → choose resolution → selectively not-see → permit play → observe consequence → restore context → learn.

The whole mind doesn’t stare at everything simultaneously.

It knows where it could look.